# Re-assert the tailnet source exemption's position in nat POSTROUTING.
#
# Fired by homelab-tailnet-source-check.timer every 15 minutes. It runs the same
# script as homelab-tailnet-source.service, which is idempotent -- it deletes
# and re-inserts the rule at position 1, then asserts the ordering.
#
# WHY THIS IS A SECOND UNIT rather than a timer pointed at the first one.
# homelab-tailnet-source.service is `RemainAfterExit=yes`, so it stays active
# after it finishes, and `systemctl start` on an already-active unit is a
# NO-OP. Measured 2026-09-22: a plain `start` left the journal untouched --
# no new invocation, no ExecStart, and a success exit. A timer wired to that
# unit would have fired on schedule, reported nothing wrong, and re-asserted
# nothing, which is the same shape as the bug it exists to catch.
#
# So this unit is deliberately NOT RemainAfterExit, and deliberately has NO
# ExecStop -- the sibling's ExecStop deletes the rule, and with
# RemainAfterExit=no that would fire the instant ExecStart returned, removing
# the rule it had just placed.
#
# WHAT IT IS FOR. On 2026-09-22 the exemption was found at POSTROUTING
# position 2, below the jump to ts-postrouting, with a packet counter of zero.
# Every remote_ip-gated vhost had been unreachable from the tailnet for at
# least seven days. The sibling unit is PartOf tailscaled and docker, so it
# re-asserts when either restarts -- but Tailscale re-inserts its own jump at
# other times too, and nothing was re-running the check to find out.
#
# See scripts/tailnet-source-rule.sh and docs/lessons-learned.md.

[Unit]
Description=Re-assert the tailnet source exemption's position in nat POSTROUTING
After=tailscaled.service docker.service
# Not PartOf: the sibling owns the rule's lifecycle. This only re-places it.
# The script exits non-zero when the ordering cannot be made correct, which is
# a real outage of every gated vhost -- alert on it like any other unit.
#
# Deliberately not rate-limited. A persistent failure alerts every 15 minutes,
# which is noisy, and this repo's own lesson is that a noisy check gets muted.
# Accepted anyway: the script re-inserts at position 1 on every run, so a
# repeating failure means something is actively re-ordering the chain faster
# than we can correct it. That is worth being woken for, and it has never
# happened. Revisit if it ever does -- and suppress it properly rather than
# muting the notification.
OnFailure=homelab-failure-notify@%n.service

[Service]
Type=oneshot
TimeoutStartSec=60
ExecStart=/usr/local/sbin/tailnet-source-rule.sh
